The subscript of a macro variable may itself be a variable, so PT1
means the permanent variable that T1 numbers. A temporary variable in
that position is what turns a run of permanent variables into an array
with a moving index. The ten that a process starts with are enough for
a small one.

[1][4][9][16][25][36]
Two subscripts at once, one rising and one falling, reverse the array
in place. The third temporary holds the element being swapped, which
is what the default capacity of three is for.

[36][25][16][9][4][1]
The array is global and outlives the call, but the index belongs to
the call. So a macro that walks the array from inside another walk of
the same array leaves the outer index where it was. An index kept in a
permanent variable would not survive this.
[36 of 4 sums to 86][25 of 4 sums to 86][16 of 4 sums to 86][9 of 4 sums to 86]
